%% ================================================================ PREFACE
\section*{The source}

\initial{P}{laude euge theotocos} survives in a single manuscript, Leipzig,
Universitätsbibliothek, Ms 1236, f. 139v. The book is a paper miscellany of
579 leaves (21.5 × 14 cm), mostly grammar, theology and philosophy, copied in
1447 and 1449; one colophon places its copying in Parchim, in Mecklenburg. It
belonged to Johannes Klein of Löbau (Lubawa, in the Chełmno land, not far from
Grudziądz), who matriculated at Leipzig in 1447, later taught in the arts
faculty, and left his books to the university's Small Princes' College when he
died in 1490.

Music fills only a few leaves. Two more pieces by Piotr come before this one,
\textit{Presulem ephebeatum} on ff. 79v–80v and the motet \textit{Pax eterna}
/ \textit{Jacob scalam} / \textit{Terribilis est locus iste} on the leaves
just before this one, and an anonymous \textit{Veni dulcis consolator} follows
on the lower half of f. 139v. A treatise on music begins on f. 140r. Of the
seventeen sources of Piotr's music copied in his lifetime, seven come from
universities, two of them from Leipzig. Klein's book is a student's copy of
the kind that carried his songs from Kraków across central Europe. It is also
an early copy, made in Piotr's lifetime. Many of his songs reached later
singers through Bohemian copies, and Paweł Gancarczyk has shown how those
copies changed them. They gave the songs new texts, altered rhythms at
cadences, and dropped or rewrote the middle voice. No other copy of
\textit{Plaude} survives to compare, but a copy this early has had less time
to drift from what Piotr wrote.

The scribe wrote the three voices one after another, each in its own block,
and labelled the upper two Discantus and Medius in the margin; the lowest
voice has the cue ‘Tenor ymni Plaude’ (the reading of \textit{ymni} is
uncertain). The notation is void mensural notation in a fast cursive hand.
The Discantus and Medius are written in semibreves and minims, with their text
copied as a continuous line under the notes rather than set note by note. The
Tenor is written almost entirely in ligatures and carries no text beyond its
cues: the incipit, ‘Te laudantes inspice’ where the second part begins, and
‘2a fi[n]alis’ and ‘2a finale’ over the second endings. No voice has a clef, a
mensuration sign or an accidental. Below the word Discantus is a small sign
shaped like a b. The scribe ruled five-line staves by hand, unevenly, and many
notes sit loosely on them. The ends of the Discantus and Medius staves run into
the gutter, and the final notes of the Discantus in both second endings cannot
be seen.

\section*{The composer}

Piotr z Grudziądza, known in Latin as Petrus Wilhelmi de Grudencz, was born in
1392 in Grudziądz (Graudenz), then in the Prussian state of the Teutonic
Order, to a family of knightly rank. He matriculated at the University of
Kraków in 1418, became bachelor of arts in 1425 and master in 1430. The later
documents place him at the court of Frederick III. In 1442, at Frankfurt am
Main, the royal chancery issued him a letter of familiarity and safe conduct.
In 1448 the bishop of Wrocław recommended him, without success, for a canonry
at Frombork. In 1452 a petition to the papal curia calls him chaplain to the
Emperor; he was probably in Rome for Frederick's coronation. Nothing is known
of him after that year. The death date of about 1480 found in older literature
rests on a supposed stay in Silesia that no document confirms. He was a
chaplain, not a member of the musical chapel, and he appears to have had no
musical duties.

His songs, or \textit{cantiones}, are short Latin pieces for two or three
voices, simple in structure and regular in rhythm. Černý showed that they grow
out of the Central European song repertory of around 1400, to which Piotr
added newer harmonic habits. They were sung mainly in university circles, at
Kraków, Vienna and Leipzig, and in the later fifteenth century by the literary
brotherhoods of the Bohemian Utraquist churches. About forty works are
attributed to him, in more than fifty sources copied between about 1420 and
1726, from Trent to Lwów and from Frankfurt to Masovia; nearly half of the
sources are Bohemian. Thirty-three works carry his signature as an acrostic,
and this piece is one of them. The manuscript names no composer, but the
initials of the opening words spell PETRVS: \textit{Plaude Euge Theotocos
Regina Virginum Salus.} Jaromír Černý included the piece in his edition of the
complete works (1993).

\section*{The poem}

Piotr wrote the texts of his songs himself; the acrostic makes that certain
here, since the words had to be found to fit the letters. The poem is a single
stanza of eight short lines in two halves. The first half greets the Virgin,
the second asks her for mercy on those who sing. Each half is sung twice, with
a different musical ending the second time. The source spells
\textit{confidencium} and \textit{misericordie}, and this edition keeps those
spellings.

\textit{Theotocos} is the Greek \textit{Theotokos,} ‘God-bearer’, the title
for Mary confirmed at the Council of Ephesus in 431. It belongs to the
Byzantine liturgy, but it was never only an Eastern word. Latin writers used
it from the Nestorian controversy onwards (John Cassian glosses it
‘theotocos, id est dei mater’), the schools met it in the Latin John of
Damascus, and Western liturgical poetry used it freely: the office antiphon
\textit{Ave o theotokos virgo Maria}, sequences such as \textit{Ave salutata ab
angelo} (‘Ave, o theotocos’), and a hymn in fifteenth-century breviaries that
prays ‘Succurre, o Theotocos’. In Piotr's poetry it is one of many Greek
borrowings, alongside \textit{ephebeatum} and \textit{pneuma eucaristiarum,}
which show off the learning of a Kraków master. I suspect it also solved a
practical problem. The acrostic needs a word beginning with T, and \textit{Dei
genitrix} would not serve.

The rest of the language is common Marian stock. \textit{Plaude} and
\textit{euge} together (‘rejoice’, ‘well done!’) appear in Jan of Jenštejn's
Visitation sequence \textit{Ave verbi Dei parens}, copied in some forty
Bohemian sources. \textit{Euge} comes from the Vulgate (‘Euge, serve bone’,
Matthew 25:21); here it supplies the E. \textit{Regina virginum} is the
antiphon's ‘Haec est regina virginum’, and rhymed Marian prayers of the
period call her the hope ‘in te confidentium’. The closing lines echo the
\textit{Salve Regina}: ‘illos tuos misericordes oculos ad nos converte’. The
title of the oldest Polish hymn, \textit{Bogurodzica}, is the same word. It
means ‘God-bearer’ and is a calque of \textit{Theotokos.}

The Tenor's cue calls the piece a \textit{hymnus}. In the medieval sense that
means any song of praise to God or the saints (Isidore: ‘hymnus est canticum
laudantium’), which the second half's \textit{Te laudantes} makes literal.
Nothing ties it to a feast or an office. It is a devotional song of the kind
sung by students, clerics and townspeople in central Europe, in school, at
home or in church.

\section*{Pitch}

Because the source has no clefs, every pitch in a modern score of this piece
is a reconstruction, and so is the vertical relation of the voices. This
edition keeps Monique Rio's reading, a final on D with B\fl{}. I tested it
against the counterpoint. Read this way, the three voices form consonances on
almost every semibreve, while moving any one voice up or down by a step
produces dissonance throughout. The flat has to apply to all three voices.
With a flat in the Discantus alone, the lower voices form tritones on nearly
every b. The key signature here is therefore editorial, like the clefs, and it
is applied to the whole score. The source has no accidentals either. The
editorial ones follow two rules of the period: voices should not sound a
tritone against each other (\textit{mi contra fa}), and a sixth that widens to
an octave at a cadence should be major. Where the two pull against each
other, or against the melodic rule \textit{una nota super la}, the critical
notes explain the choice. The recording by Schola Gregoriana Pragensis decides
differently in three places, and those are noted too.

At this pitch the Discantus lies between c′ and b\fl{}′, the Medius between c
and c′, and the Tenor between G and g. The piece suits alto, tenor and bass,
and there is no need to transpose it.

\section*{The Tenor: sung or played?}

Both recordings of the piece treat the Tenor as something other than a sung
text. La Morra (2016) gives it to a fiddle; in the recording by Schola
Gregoriana Pragensis (2005) a bass hums it. The name of the voice does not
settle whether that is right. In the fifteenth century \textit{tenor} named a
function, the voice on which the others were built, and not a range; in
three-voice songs of this period the lowest voice is regularly the Tenor. The
other two names are local. Central European treatises of Piotr's time, the
Mazovian \textit{Circa artem} and Paulus Paulirinus, describe pieces in which
the \textit{discantus} and \textit{medium} have text while the tenor has none
and moves in longer values. That is exactly the layout of this page. Nor can
its low range be pressed, since without clefs the absolute pitch is an
editor's choice. All that is certain is that it lies about an octave below
the Discantus.

The notation is better evidence. The Tenor has no text, and it is written
almost entirely in ligatures, each of which carries a single syllable. The
scribe did not expect anyone to sing the words to it syllable by syllable. Its
long values fit an instrument well, and instruments were at hand: Paulus
Paulirinus, writing in Prague about 1460, describes the fiddle, lute,
clavichord and organ. Reinhard Strohm notes that the three-part songs of the
Glogau songbook could be played on three string instruments, sung, or mixed.

The case for voices is just as strong. Since the work of Christopher Page and
David Fallows in the late 1970s and early 1980s, many scholars have held that
much of this repertory was sung throughout. Page argues that vocalisation,
singing a textless line without words, was a standard resource of trained
singers, all the more because instruments other than the organ were normally
kept out of church. He also observes that in sources of the early fifteenth
century lower voices increasingly carry partial text, generally at moments of
imitation or declamation. This Tenor carries text cues for both halves and for
the endings, as if its reader was meant to follow the words.

Both ways of performing it have history behind them, and so does a texted
tenor. Gancarczyk shows that a copyist of the anonymous Central European motet
\textit{Ave coronata} divided the long notes of its textless tenor into short
values so that it could sing the words of the discantus, blurring the original
design. Copyists of Piotr's own songs adjusted the tenor's rhythm at section
endings, lengthening a note or adding a minim. This edition prints a texted
Tenor as an option for an all-vocal performance. It follows Page's observation
rather than the \textit{Ave coronata} copyist, so the Tenor takes text mainly
where the upper voices declaim together.

The Tenor's text is printed in italics because none of it is in the source.
The Tenor keeps its long notes so that it can hold a sustained line under the
declaiming upper voices, and it sings only as much of the text as fits:
‘Plaude theotocos / regina virginum / salus confidencium’, and ‘Te laudantes
inspice / nec despice / sed hos oculis respice’. The words it leaves out are
sung by the upper voices, and what it sings still makes sense on its own.
Notes are divided only in the endings, and dashed ties mark the divisions. The
two endings of the first part differ. The first time, the Tenor begins the
repeated \textit{confidencium} half a bar early, with \textit{con-fi} in two
minims, and puts its stress, \textit{-den-}, on the downbeat of bar 15 while
the upper voices sing \textit{con-}. The second time, it holds its breve and
then takes the rhythm and words of the upper voices, four minims into the
final breve. In the first ending of the second part the breve on d is halved
to carry \textit{respice}. The Tenor can equally be vocalised or played from
this score.

