\section*{Editorial method}

\subsection{Pitch and clefs} The pitch is that described above, written in
modern treble, octave-treble and bass clefs. The key signature is editorial.

\subsection{Note values and barring} The note values are those of the source
(semibreve = whole note). Lines between the staves mark every breve, and the
staves themselves are left without barlines, so notes run across them as they
do in the source. Bar numbers count breves and include both endings. The
source has no mensuration sign. The music moves in imperfect tempus and minor
prolation throughout.

\subsection{Repeats} The source writes each half once, followed by its first
ending, and then the second ending after a double stroke marked ‘2a’. The
score uses repeat signs and numbered endings for this. In the first part the
bar before the endings (bar 14) is printed in both endings, so that the Tenor
can take it differently each time; the upper voices are the same in both. In
the source the endings begin one bar later.

\subsection{Accidentals} The source has none. Editorial accidentals stand
above the note they affect, in small type, and apply to that note only. There
are four: naturals in bars 20 and 30, and two sharps in the final cadence,
bar 35. The critical notes give the reasons, and note where \sig{R} and
\sig{P} differ.

\subsection{Supplied notes} Notes hidden in the gutter of the manuscript are
supplied in square brackets.

\subsection{Text} Spelling follows the source; capitals and punctuation are
editorial. The source does not align syllables with notes in any voice, so the
underlay throughout is editorial. It follows the phrase structure. Each line
of the poem ends at a cadence, with its last syllable on the arrival note: in
the second part, \textit{inspice} at the cadence on A (bar 24),
\textit{despice} on G (bar 27) and \textit{oculis} on C (bar 31), the first
note of each ending. Stressed syllables fall on the beat and usually on longer
notes (\textit{laudántes, ínspice, míseros, déspice, misericórdie, óculis,
réspice}). A repeated note takes a new syllable. Melismas fall on the stressed
or the penultimate syllable before a cadence. Where the upper voices move
together, they declaim together. I took these rules from sixteenth-century
theorists, writing a century after this music, and applied them only where
they make musical sense. The Tenor's text is wholly editorial, printed in
italics, and shortened so that it keeps its long notes (see above).

\subsection{Final notes} The source writes the last note of each ending as a
long in the Tenor, and in the Medius at the end of each second ending. They
are printed as longs, each filling its bar, and are held until all voices
have finished. No fermata is added.

